(>> 5.1 Object-Oriented Programming Paradigm Principles) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Object-Oriented Programming \(OOP\) models software systems around real-world entities) Tj
T*
(combining data state \(attributes\) and behavioral operations \(methods\):) Tj
T*
(* Four Cardinal Pillars of OOP: \(1\) Encapsulation: Bundling data and methods into a) Tj
T*
(single class unit while restricting direct external access to internal state. \(2\)) Tj
T*
(Abstraction: Exposing public interfaces while hiding complex implementation details.) Tj
T*
(\(3\) Inheritance: Creating hierarchical classes that inherit and extend attributes and) Tj
T*
(methods from base classes. \(4\) Polymorphism: Presenting a unified interface for) Tj
T*
(entities of different underlying types.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
